[38;5;252m[1m## Things worth knowing about the test design[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The two libraries get different [38;5;10mmalloc[0m addresses, so instead of comparing raw pointers, [38;5;10mDual::check[0m compares — after [3mevery single call[23m — the array header, the entire [38;5;10mstbds_hash_index[0m, every [38;5;10mbucket.hash[][0m/[38;5;10mbucket.index[][0m slot, the element key material, and a per-insert payload tag that makes [38;5;10mhmdel_key[0m's element [38;5;10mmemmove[0m observable.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The strongest layout evidence is [38;5;10mrow75[0m/[38;5;10mrow75b[0m: a table built by C's [38;5;10mhmput_key[0m is queried and drained by Rust's [38;5;10mhmget_key[0m/[38;5;10mhmdel_key[0m, and vice versa. That only works if header, hash index, bucket layout, both hash functions and the probe sequence are bit-identical.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Three C behaviours I replicated rather than fixed, and tested explicitly:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mhmput_key[0m writes [38;5;10mtable->temp_key[0m when a duplicate is found in the upper bucket sub-loop but [1mnot[22m in the wrap-around sub-loop. [38;5;10merr26_27[0m proves this by poisoning [38;5;10mtemp_key[0m with a sentinel before re-putting each of 4000 keys and asserting both libraries classify every key into the same branch, with both branches non-empty.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mhmdel_key[0m gates its string handling on [38;5;10mmode == 1[0m exactly while hashing uses [38;5;10mmode >= 1[0m, so [38;5;10mmode == 2[0m hashes as a string and re-finds as binary.[0m[0m
